  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nMost mornings we bring up the nighttime microphysics RGB as the \nproduct to look at, mainly because it helps us pick out areas of low \nlevel cloud cover and fog, and while that is also the case today I'd \nlike to put some focus on the Differential Water Vapor RGB. There is \na tremendous signal between the clouds and moisture flowing \nsoutheast of the area down where there are still showers ongoing in \neastern Iowa through southern WI and northern IL and where there is \na bit of dry air just northwest of the this line as evident by the \ntan/brown. Northwest of the thin line of dry air is air that once \nagain contains a bit more moisture despite the lack of cloud cover, \nmainly due to the subsidence as high pressure moves in. Our lower \nlevel temperatures from the surface to around 850mb continues to be \non the warmer side, especially for this time of year, which will \nallow temperatures to reach into the mid 70s this afternoon with \nsome 80s in western MN possible in our normal warm spots. This will \nbe relatively short lived as after today we should drop back into \nthe 60s for high for the rest of the period, with lows likewise \ndropping from near 60 this morning to mid to upper 50s tomorrow \nmorning down to the upper 40s by Friday morning. Keep in mind that \nfor Sep 30th for the Twin Cities, our normal high is 67 and low is \n48; these numbers will only continue to fall into the first week of \nOctober so we will be essentially near normal temperature wise \ndespite it seeming colder simply due to having been warmer than \nnormal recently. Taking another queue from climatology, our average \nfirst 32 or colder day in the Twin Cities is October 12th and the \nlonger range guidance favors continued 60s for highs and 40s for \nlows through the first half of October, meaning we probably end up \non the later side for a first hard freeze. Whether or not we make a \nrun at the latest on record being Nov 18th back in 2016 remains to \nbe seen.\n\nThe main thing to look at besides the temperatures moving forward \nwill be a chance for showers on Saturday evening as a shortwave \nquickly sweeps through the region, with accumulation generally a \ntenth of an inch or less with the current forecast. The GFS suite is \nmuch drier than the ECMWF, however even the most bullish EPS members \nonly show a couple tenths of an inch at the most with duration of \nprecip only 3 to 4 hours as the shortwave rapidly moves through. GFS \nforecast soundings show a classic inverted V lower level profile, \nalthough lapse rates become poor above 850mb such that instability \nis minimized. We could still manage to squeeze out some thunder \ngiven the degree of surface instability that would be possible, with \nthe main question being how well we can saturate the lower levels to \novercome the weak lapse rates. For now, don't expect much in the way \nof thunder, with some isolated to scattered light showers being the \nexpectation. After Saturday we resume our dry pattern as a broad \narea of high pressure drops down into the northern plains and into \nthe Great Lakes to start next week.",
